.\" Manpage for sysrq-on-shutdown.
.TH SYSRQ-ON-SHUTDOWN 8 "2026-10-16" "sysrq-on-shutdown 1.0.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
sysrq-on-shutdown \- enable full SysRq keys during shutdown and reboot
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B systemctl
.RI enable " sysrq\-on\-shutdown.service"
.br
.B systemctl
.RI start " sysrq\-on\-shutdown.service"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sysrq-on-shutdown.service
is a oneshot unit that writes
.B 1
to
.IR /proc/sys/kernel/sysrq .
This enables every SysRq command for the remainder of the shutdown or reboot
sequence, so that the magic key combinations
.RB ( Alt + SysRq + key ,
or a character written to
.IR /proc/sysrq-trigger )
keep working while the system is bringing services and storage down.
.PP
Many distributions ship a restricted default value for
.IR kernel.sysrq ,
which masks some commands such as
.BR b " (reboot), " o " (power off) and " s " (sync)."
On Arch Linux the kernel default is
.BR 16 ,
which enables only the
.B sync
command. By raising the value to
.B 1
immediately before
.BR shutdown.target ,
the emergency key set stays available for the whole shutdown path.
.PP
The unit is ordered
.B Before=shutdown.target
and built with
.BR DefaultDependencies=no ,
so it runs early in the shutdown transaction, after the regular
.B shutdown.target
dependencies are no longer required.
.SH ENABLING
The service is intentionally not enabled on installation. Enable it explicitly:
.PP
.nf
    # systemctl enable sysrq-on-shutdown.service
.fi
.PP
To also apply it to the current boot without waiting for a reboot:
.PP
.nf
    # systemctl start sysrq-on-shutdown.service
.fi
.PP
Check the current value at any time with:
.PP
.nf
    $ cat /proc/sys/kernel/sysrq
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/sysrq-on-shutdown.service
The systemd unit installed by this package.
.TP
.I /proc/sys/kernel/sysrq
Runtime control of the SysRq key set; see
.BR proc_sysrq (2)
and
.BR sysrq (2)
in the kernel documentation
.RI ( Documentation/admin\-guide/sysrq.rst ).
.TP
.I /etc/sysctl.d/*.conf
Persistent SysRq configuration. A local override such as
.B kernel.sysrq = 1
achieves a similar effect for the entire run time, whereas this unit only
raises the value while shutting down.
.SH SECURITY
Enabling the full SysRq set lets any user with access to the console (or root)
force a reboot, kill processes, or dump kernel state without a clean unmount.
Enabling this unit only for the shutdown path limits that window to shutdown and
reboot, which is the intended trade-off.
.SH EXAMPLES
Enable the hook and verify it is queued for shutdown:
.PP
.nf
    # systemctl enable sysrq-on-shutdown.service
    # systemctl status sysrq-on-shutdown.service
.fi
.PP
Trigger the full SysRq set manually for testing:
.PP
.nf
    # echo 1 > /proc/sys/kernel/sysrq
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR systemd.special (7),
.BR systemd.service (5),
.BR proc_sysrq (2),
.BR sysrq (2),
.BR sysctl.d (5)
